From c85a484863cae1e0d80ee5347ffb74f6cb2f8c30 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 08:14:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 086/145] media: meson: vdec: hevc: resync at an IRAP after a
 flush too

codec_hevc_flush_output() frees every frame on the reference list, but
leaves hevc->seen_irap set. seen_irap is assigned in exactly one place -
set to 1 on the first IRAP of a session - and nothing ever clears it, so
after a flush the decoder believes it still has a valid reference chain
when in fact it has none.

This is the drain op, which the core calls on CAPTURE STREAMOFF, and a
seek is precisely that: userspace stops both queues, restarts them, and
resumes feeding from a new position mid-GOP. The first frames after the
seek reference pictures that were freed a moment ago, so every lookup
fails and the substitution path rebuilds them from whatever frame is
nearest.

The user-visible effect is that seeking around a stream corrupts it even
when nothing in the stream is damaged - which is how this was found:
skipping through a 4K HDR clip destabilised the board well before reaching
the one genuinely malformed frame it contains.

Clear seen_irap and curr_poc alongside the frames they describe. The
decoder then skips slices until the next IRAP, exactly as it already does
for a session that starts mid-GOP, and resumes cleanly with a real
reference chain.

Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
---
 drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c
index bea2cd1b42e0..1d93e451cce0 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc.c
@@ -778,6 +778,21 @@ static void codec_hevc_flush_output(struct amvdec_session *sess)
 		list_del(&tmp->list);
 		kfree(tmp);
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Every reference this session held has just been freed, so nothing
+	 * arriving next can be decoded until the stream restarts at an IRAP.
+	 *
+	 * This runs as the drain op, which the core calls on CAPTURE
+	 * STREAMOFF - and a seek is exactly that: userspace stops both
+	 * queues, restarts them, and resumes feeding from a new position
+	 * mid-GOP.  Without clearing seen_irap the decoder carried straight
+	 * on, every frame failed its reference lookup, and the substitution
+	 * path reconstructed pictures from whatever was nearest - so seeking
+	 * around a stream corrupted it even when no frame in it was damaged.
+	 */
+	hevc->seen_irap = 0;
+	hevc->curr_poc = INVALID_POC;
 }
 
 static int codec_hevc_stop(struct amvdec_session *sess)
